articles.content_en:
Article 1 Where the labor dispute arbitration commission refuses to accept a labor dispute case on the ground that it has no jurisdiction, and the party initiates a lawsuit, the People's Court shall handle the case according to the following circumstances:
(1) If after examination it finds that the labor dispute arbitration commission indeed has no jurisdiction over the case, it shall inform the party to apply for arbitration with the labor dispute arbitration commission that has jurisdiction;
(2) If after examination it finds that the labor dispute arbitration commission has jurisdiction, it shall inform the party to apply for arbitration and notify the labor dispute arbitration commission in writing of its examination opinion. If the labor dispute arbitration commission still refuses to accept the case, and the party initiates a lawsuit regarding the labor dispute, the court shall accept the case.

Article 5 Where a worker is assigned from the original employer to work for a new employer for reasons not attributable to the worker himself, and the original employer has not paid economic compensation, and the worker terminates the labor contract with the new employer in accordance with Article 38 of the Labor Contract Law, or the new employer proposes to rescind or terminate the labor contract, when calculating the working years for payment of economic compensation or damages, if the worker requests that the working years with the original employer be combined with the working years with the new employer, the People's Court shall support such request.
If the employer falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall be deemed that "the worker is assigned from the original employer to work for the new employer for reasons not attributable to the worker himself":
(1) The worker continues to work at the same workplace and in the same position, but the subject of the labor contract changes from the original employer to the new employer;
(2) The employer transfers the worker by way of organizational assignment or appointment;
(3) The worker's transfer results from merger, division or other reasons of the employer;
(4) The employer and its affiliated enterprises sign labor contracts with the worker in turn;
(5) Other reasonable circumstances.
